#X text 20 1300 The x24 and the wrap~ rebuild u_tempo's pulse off this instance's own phasor \, so two clocks at the same rate fire in the same block by construction (ref/module/tempo.md)., f 58;
#X text 20 1500 One phasor for the signal outlet and the events both: a second oscillator would drift from the first., f 58;
#X text 20 1630 A signal fan-out needs no trigger. C-3 is for messages., f 58;
#X text 900 1300 Start resets \, stop does not halt: halting is the consumer's business. The start reset is all that aligns instances to each other and to master., f 44;
#X text 900 1480 Four receives on one name \, all into cold inlets \, so their order does not matter. The next pulse is then the first of the bar., f 44;
#X text 900 1660 The bar chain runs first \, off the right outlet \, so a consumer reading beat-in-bar on the beat gets this beat., f 44;
#X text 1660 660 Bad arguments are corrected at load and reported 2 s later. C-9, f 44;
#X text 1660 840 moses' left outlet carries the unmatched value \, harmless only because it feeds a message box. C-8, f 44;
#X text 1660 1160 Debounces are 0: threshold~ counts dead time in blocks \, so any debounce loses pulses (item 58). The ratio multiplies the ceiling \, so at 1 the pulses hold to about 860 BPM of u_tempo's 1000 clamp, f 44;
#X obj 20 330 f 120;
#X obj 400 290 loadbang;
#X obj 400 330 del 300;
#X text 700 240 Seeds its own tempo at 300 ms: u_tempo publishes once at load \, so an instance born later would otherwise sit at 0 Hz (ref/module/tempo.md)., f 50;
